Z||000016||terryk||07-12-2001||10:45 PM||||So is it 10's or 12's? They are a world apart. A 12 second car will pass as a daily driver if it's blown. A 10s car won't unless you are on the constant verge of driving  over the crank.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Shaun41178||07-13-2001||12:30 AM||||The bowtie aluminum block still uses the same bearings as the stock block does.  It just comes with 4 bolt splayed billet steal intermediate main caps, V8 style oil galleries for improved oil effiency to the lower half of the engine and weighs much less then the iron one<P>The aluminum block can be bored .060 over no problem and with a 3.1 crank will make a 3.3 liter.  Although if you use aluminum heads it will probably drop to a 3.2 since the aluminum heads don't have as many cc's as the iron ones  All aluminum will drop about 80 or more pounds out of the rear of the Fiero.  Thats equal to about .1 in the qtr mile right there<P>fldevel if you want to come take a look at my block I live in Daytona Beach Florida.  I don't know where Lecanto is but if it is close drive on over and take a look at it.  You won't be dissapointed at its beauty.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
