Z||000021||Ken Wittlief||07-13-2001||10:39 AM||||personally I wouldnt put that kinda HP in anything but an '88 - you need suspension and brakes to match what you are putting under the deck lid - otherwise you will end up being an organ donor<P> if you start with an '88 you will be half way there - if you hotrod and '84 you will end up spending more in the long run<P>  just my 2 cents.||||reg||1
Z||000022||KRMFiero||07-13-2001||05:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>personally I wouldnt put that kinda HP in anything but an '88 - you need suspension and brakes to match what you are putting under the deck lid - otherwise you will end up being an organ donor<P> if you start with an '88 you will be half way there - if you hotrod and '84 you will end up spending more in the long run<P>  just my 2 cents.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldnt but anything but a Stock Fiero engine in a 88, I just think that everything about them stock is nice, plus if any fiero is going to be worth money someday (there is a chance) it will probley be a 88....  I would do all the conversions on 84-87's  <BR>Kyle<P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||07-13-2001||05:15 PM||||ok you got me on that one<P> acutally I wouldnt dump alot of money into making a Fiero into a rocket sled at all - I think the V6 versions are pretty decent fun little sports cars just the way they are<P>  and there is 'something' special about a car like this that is still stock - and is a blast to drive - not that Im a pureist or anything - its tough to find a car right from the factory that doesnt need 'a few minor adjustments'  :c)<P> and if I wanted to build a 400 or 500HP monster - I can think of lots of other cars I would choose first before getting to the Fiero.<BR>   <BR> But hey - to each his own.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
